---
const getIconUrl = (slug: string) => `https://cdn.simpleicons.org/${slug}`;

const backend = [
  { name: 'Go', slug: 'go' },
  { name: 'TypeScript', slug: 'typescript' },
  { name: 'Python', slug: 'python' },
];

const machineLearning = [
  { name: 'Jupyter', slug: 'jupyter' },
  { name: 'SkLearn', slug: 'scikitlearn' },
  { name: 'Pandas', slug: 'Pandas' },
  { name: 'Numpy', slug: 'numpy' },
];

const infra = [
  { name: 'Docker', slug: 'docker' },
  { name: 'Cloudflare', slug: 'cloudflare' },
  { name: 'Github', slug: 'github' },
  { name: 'Linux', slug: 'linux' },
];

const systems = [
  { name: 'Go', slug: 'go' },
  { name: 'OCaml', slug: 'ocaml'},
  { name: 'C++', slug: 'cplusplus' },
  { name: 'Linux', slug: 'linux' },
];
---

<section class="skills-wrapper">
  <h2>Tech I like</h2>

  <!-- Backend -->
  <div class="category-block">
    <h4>Backend</h4>
      <p>
		I feel most comfortable with strongly typed systems
		languages. <a href="https://go.dev/">Go</a> is my go-to (hah!) for writing backend
		services. Great DX, tooling, and resources.
	  </p>
    <div class="skills-grid">
      {backend.map((skill) => (
        <div class="skill-item">
          <img src={getIconUrl(skill.slug)} alt={skill.name} class="skill-icon" />
          <span>{skill.name}</span>
        </div>
      ))}

      <div class="skill-item">
        <img src="https://img.icons8.com/?size=100&id=2572&format=png&color=f89820"
        alt="Java" class="skill-icon" />
        <span>Java</span>
      </div>
	  
    </div>

	<p>TypeScript and Java can be very auto-magical, and that is pretty great when it
	works, but I find that I often like to understand everything going on inside my web
	services, allows me to get up and running with a new framework quicker.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- ML -->
  <div class="category-block">
    <h4>Machine Learning</h4>
    <p>
	  My concentration in Advanced AI for Data Science was truly eye-opening. Jupyter is a
	  great tool for literate programming and allowed me to create great, reproducible
	  reports with all the great open-source python tools in the area.
	</p>
    <div class="skills-grid">
      {machineLearning.map((skill) => (
        <div class="skill-item">
          <img src={getIconUrl(skill.slug)} alt={skill.name} class="skill-icon" />
          <span>{skill.name}</span>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Infra -->
  <div class="category-block">
    <h4>Infrastructure & Tools</h4>
	<p>
	  Of course, I like easily reproducible environments, and containarized applications
	  and serverless platforms are the best way I know to deploy software. Sprinkle some
	  Github Action's for CI/CD and you have it all up and running, just like in your dev
	  machine.
	</p>
    <div class="skills-grid">
      {infra.map((skill) => (
        <div class="skill-item">
          <img src={getIconUrl(skill.slug)} alt={skill.name} class="skill-icon" />
          <span>{skill.name}</span>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Systems -->
  <div class="category-block">
    <h4>Systems programming</h4>
	<p>
	  I don't think it matters what layer of the stack you work on, or how far removed
	  your code is from the metal it eventually runs on, every engineer benefits from
	  doing small experiments and learning about the low level systems the world runs on.
	</p>
    <div class="skills-grid">
      {systems.map((skill) => (
        <div class="skill-item">
          <img src={getIconUrl(skill.slug)} alt={skill.name} class="skill-icon" />
          <span>{skill.name}</span>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .skills-wrapper {
	padding-top: 3rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 3rem;
  }
  .category-block h4 {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    font-size: 1.4rem;
  }
  .category-block p {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    max-width: 60ch;
	font-size: 1.1rem;
  }
  .skills-grid {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 1rem;
  }
  .skill-item {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    padding: 0.4rem 0.8rem;
    border-radius: 8px;
  }
  .skill-icon {
    width: 32px;
    height: 32px;
  }
</style>
